==Reactions==
Margvelashvili was, at the time, an ally of Prime Minister [[Bidzina Ivanishvili]], and the result was considered to be a consolidation of Ivanishvili's power.<ref name=observers/> With his appointee elected to the presidency, Ivanishvili declared his intention to leave his role as Prime Minister, stating that his goals had been achieved.<ref name=BBC_1>{{cite news|title=Georgia PM ally Giorgi Margvelashvili 'wins presidency'|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-24696261|accessdate=28 October 2013|newspaper=BBC News|date=27 October 2013}}</ref>

The election was declared as "clean" and "transparent" by international observers.<ref name=observers>{{cite news|last=Berry|first=Lynn|title=International Observers Praise Georgia's Election|url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/georgian-president-elected-62-percent-20699954|accessdate=28 October 2013|agency=Associated Press|date=28 October 2013}}</ref> The [[OSCE]] observer mission preliminary report stated that the election was "efficiently administered, transparent and took place in an amicable and constructive environment."<ref>{{cite web|title=Fundamental freedoms respected in well-administered presidential election in Georgia, international observers say|url=http://www.osce.org/odihr/elections/107512|publisher=OSCE|accessdate=29 October 2013|date=27 October 2013}}</ref> However, there were some issues noted by observers. International Society for Fair Elections and Democracy, a Georgian election observer group, found "significant shortcomings" regarding invalid ID and voter lists in [[Batumi]], filing 45 complaints. Another group, [[Transparency International]], filed 34 complaints.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=26626|title=Margvelashvili Set for Outright Victory|author=Civil Georgia|date=28 October 2013|publisher=Civil.ge|accessdate=28 October 2013}}</ref>

===International responses===
;Supranational organizations 
*{{flagu|European Union}} — [[Catherine Ashton]], the EU High Representative for Foreign Affairs, and [[Štefan Füle]], the Commissioner for Enlargement and European Neighbourhood Policy, released a statement where they congratulated "the Georgian people on this demonstration of their country's strong democratic credentials" and that they "look forward to continued close cooperation with Georgia on our ambitious mutual agenda of political association and economic integration."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://europa.eu/rapid/press-release_MEMO-13-940_en.htm?locale=en|title=Joint Statement by EU High Representative Catherine Ashton and Commissioner Štefan Füle on the results of Georgia's presidential election|author=European Commission|date=28 October 2013|publisher=Europa.eu|accessdate=28 October 2013}}</ref>
;States
*{{flagu|United States}} — US Department of State release said that they "witnessed another historic day for all Georgians" and an "important step in Georgia's democratic development and its embrace of Euro-Atlantic institutions."<ref>{{cite web|title=Georgian Presidential Election|url=https://2009-2017.state.gov/r/pa/prs/ps/2013/10/215929.htm|publisher=U.S. State Department|accessdate=28 October 2013|date=28 October 2013}}</ref>
*{{flagu|Russia}} — Russian Foreign Minister [[Sergey Lavrov]] congratulated the Georgian people on the "free and fair election" and expressed his hope that the new government would "adopt a friendly policy toward Russia, one that will take into account the current situation in this complex region."<ref>{{cite news|last=Nechepurenko|first=Ivan|title=With Saakashvili Out, Russia-Georgia Ties Set to Improve, but Slowly|url=http://www.themoscowtimes.com/news/article/with-saakashvili-out-russia-georgia-ties-set-to-improve-but-slowly/488642.html|accessdate=28 October 2013|newspaper=[[The Moscow Times]]|date=28 October 2013}}</ref>
*{{flagu|Sweden}} — Foreign Minister [[Carl Bildt]] congratulated Georgia on "its well-run presidential election and Giorgi Margvelashvili on clearly being the winner."<ref>{{cite web|title=Carl Bildt|url=https://twitter.com/carlbildt/status/394776064456945664|publisher=Twitter|accessdate=28 October 2013}}</ref>

